A||||8||Cooter||Help for a friend with a tapping Duke?||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||03-01-2001||11:03 PM||||One of my friends has an 87 SE with just over 100,000 miles on it. When he bought it, it had a cracked head from overheating. He replaced the head and the car runs great. But it has valve train noises. So, since there is no adjustment, we assumed that a lifter must be slacking. The new lifters did not help. So we noticed the valve springs were very very weak, so they were replaced but the tapping is still there. It most definately in the valve train, but we are out of ideas. The car runs super smooth and has more power than my 50,000 mile Duck-Mobile did. Is there something else that we could check?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||03-01-2001||11:15 PM||||I cant remember, but if the duke has"non roller" lifters then installing new lifters will in a way, wear out the cam even more.  Thats what i have read, and been told.<P><BR>Did you adjust the lifter rods?  I know on most cars you have to tighten the nut on the rocker until the rods has a slight drag.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||03-02-2001||02:03 AM||||What kind of engine are you talking about? Hyd. lifters can always be adjusted, for that matter all lifters can be adjusted.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||03-02-2001||03:38 PM||||Oh- it is a Duke with rollers. If you know how to adjust the lifter preload, please share it with us because it really sounds like they are just a little loose.||||reg||1
Z||000004||862M4inCA||03-02-2001||04:09 PM||||Hrm....something I learned when working on my valve train is that there is a slight difference in size between the 87 and 86 pushrods (like .040" longer for 86), and that slack is taken up using a shorter rocker arm bolt. I learned the hard way by using 86 pushrods and 87 bolts. LOL Punched thru about 3 rockers before I realized what I'd done. Also is it at all possible that perhaps the cam has developed a flat spot? I'm having a similar problem with mine right now (tho I think I induced it myself by possibly mixing up my lifters when reinstalling them even tho I had them marked I got in a hurry and misread my markings). I'm also wondering if it's at all possible that you got a bad lifter in the bunch, pro'lly unlikely, but always a possibility.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||03-02-2001||08:29 PM||||I'm pulling some of this out of the air . . .<P>Since rocker arm pivot bolts screw solidly into the cylinder head, it looks like valve stem height is critical.  Maybe the valve stems were ground too short.<P>I don't know what you can do about this.  Longer pushrods, as noted above?  Shims between the bolt head and the rocker arm pivot ball?  Just wild ideas.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-02-2001||11:17 PM||||Actually rockers in a bunch of GM motors can Not be adjusted. The hardware is setup to stop at a particular point. The only hope for these is changing some parts or Maybe, just Maybe, a thin shim in the right place. a couple thousandths is probably all thats needed.<P>My own motor also does this. I've seen a couple others as well. I have not had the time and good weather to look into this. It's bloody anoying. As soon as I find anything out I'll let every one know. I think there is something up with the OE setup in this head. It was the first year for a major rework of the whole motor.<P>What I'm hoping is that I can either rob parts or a whole head off a later version of the DIS motor and fix the valve noise. (My guides and seals are starting to get crappy so I'll have to figure this out in the semi near future. Right now I'm busy with brakes.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||03-02-2001||11:53 PM||||OK, I see now, you have a 4 cylinder engine, not a V-6.<BR>Some things to check are the top end on the valve stem to make sure that they are not mushroomed out. Next thing is to check the rocker arms themselves for abnormal wear and flat spots along with the valve spring tension. If you don't want to do a tear down look into som rockers for solid rollers, they normally have an adjustment screw for the pushrod side. Like the high performance stuff for small block V-8's||||reg||1
Z||000008||Kelvin Vivian||03-03-2001||01:02 AM||||Make sure that you are not missing any bolts back there. One time I heard tapping noises from the rear and I was missing some water pump bracket bolts, or something like that, I took it into Goodyear, they saw the problem and replaced the missing bolts for free...<P>Why free? They make their living off of servicing my mother's car...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck,<BR>Kelvin<BR>87 SE 4-cyl, medium red met/grey, 5-spd<BR>88 GT T-top, bright red/tan, 5-spd||||reg||1
